Tier 5 counter verification: the 87 hardwired-zero mhpm* CSRs (range decode, including both sides of the >=3 boundary), mcycleh/minstreth existence and half-write isolation, and the 32-to-64 bit carry -- tested at BOTH polarities, because mcycle carries on a cycle where nothing retires and minstret must not. Also confirms WFI decodes as a legal NOP. STANDALONE - swap this in alone, do not combine with testcodes/6,7,8,9,10,11,12,13.
m4_define(['m4_asm_ECALL'],  ['32'b00000000000000000000000001110011'])
m4_define(['m4_asm_EBREAK'], ['32'b00000000000100000000000001110011'])
m4_define(['m4_asm_MRET'],   ['32'b00110000001000000000000001110011'])
m4_define(['m4_asm_WFI'],    ['32'b00010000010100000000000001110011'])
m4_asm(ADDI,  x1,  x0,  10100100)            //   0  x1 = 164 (handler address)
m4_asm(CSRRW, x0,  x1,  1100000101)          //   4  mtvec = 164
m4_asm(ADDI,  x2,  x0,  111111111111)        //   8  x2 = -1 = 0xFFFFFFFF (rollover seed)
m4_asm(ADDI,  x3,  x0,  100000000)           //  12  x3 = 0x100
m4_asm(ADDI,  x4,  x0,  1010101011)          //  16  x4 = 0x2AB

   // ---- A: the mhpm* blocks must read zero and must NOT trap ----
m4_asm(CSRRS, x5,  x0,  101100000011)        //  20  mhpmcounter3   (0xB03)
m4_asm(CSRRS, x6,  x0,  101100011111)        //  24  mhpmcounter31  (0xB1F)
m4_asm(CSRRS, x7,  x0,  101110000011)        //  28  mhpmcounterh3  (0xB83)
m4_asm(CSRRS, x8,  x0,  101110011111)        //  32  mhpmcounterh31 (0xB9F)
m4_asm(CSRRS, x9,  x0,  1100100011)          //  36  mhpmevent3     (0x323)
m4_asm(CSRRS, x10, x0,  1100111111)          //  40  mhpmevent31    (0x33F)
m4_asm(CSRRW, x0,  x2,  101100000011)        //  44  write 0xFFFFFFFF to 0xB03 -- legal, dropped
m4_asm(CSRRS, x11, x0,  101100000011)        //  48  x11 = 0xB03, still 0 (WARL hardwired zero)

   // ---- A': the >= 3 boundary -- one address below each block must still TRAP ----
m4_asm(ADDI,  x12, x0,  1000101011)          //  52  x12 = 555 canary
m4_asm(ADDI,  x13, x0,  1000101011)          //  56  x13 = 555 canary
m4_asm(CSRRS, x12, x0,  101100000001)        //  60  0xB01 reserved -> TRAP 1, cause 2
m4_asm(CSRRS, x13, x0,  1100100000)          //  64  0x320 mcountinhibit -> TRAP 2, cause 2

   // ---- B: the high halves exist, and a write hits ONLY the addressed half ----
m4_asm(CSRRW, x0,  x3,  101100000000)        //  68  mcycle  <= 0x100
m4_asm(CSRRW, x0,  x4,  101110000000)        //  72  mcycleh <= 0x2AB (mcycle ticks to 0x101)
m4_asm(CSRRS, x14, x0,  101100000000)        //  76  x14 = mcycle  = 0x101 -- low half survived
m4_asm(CSRRS, x15, x0,  101110000000)        //  80  x15 = mcycleh = 0x2AB
m4_asm(CSRRW, x0,  x4,  101110000010)        //  84  minstreth <= 0x2AB
m4_asm(CSRRS, x16, x0,  101110000010)        //  88  x16 = minstreth = 0x2AB

   // ---- C1: mcycle rollover on a cycle that DOES retire ----
m4_asm(CSRRW, x0,  x2,  101100000000)        //  92  mcycle <= 0xFFFFFFFF (a WRITE: must not carry)
m4_asm(ADDI,  x17, x0,  1)                   //  96  retiring instruction; THIS is the rollover cycle
m4_asm(CSRRS, x18, x0,  101110000000)        // 100  x18 = mcycleh = 0x2AC

   // ---- C2: mcycle rollover on a cycle that does NOT retire -- the load-bearing probe ----
m4_asm(CSRRW, x0,  x2,  101100000000)        // 104  mcycle <= 0xFFFFFFFF
m4_asm(ECALL)                                // 108  TRAP 3, cause 11. Does not retire, but the
                                             //      CYCLE still happened, so mcycle must roll over
m4_asm(CSRRS, x26, x0,  101110000000)        // 112  x26 = mcycleh = 0x2AD

   // ---- C3: a WRITE of 0xFFFFFFFF is not a rollover ----
m4_asm(CSRRW, x0,  x2,  101100000000)        // 116  mcycle <= 0xFFFFFFFF
m4_asm(CSRRW, x0,  x3,  101100000000)        // 120  mcycle_old is 0xFFFFFFFF but this is a WRITE
m4_asm(CSRRS, x27, x0,  101110000000)        // 124  x27 = mcycleh = 0x2AD, unchanged

   // ---- D1: minstret rollover on a retiring instruction ----
m4_asm(CSRRW, x0,  x2,  101100000010)        // 128  minstret <= 0xFFFFFFFF
m4_asm(ADDI,  x28, x0,  1)                   // 132  retires -> minstret rolls over
m4_asm(CSRRS, x29, x0,  101110000010)        // 136  x29 = minstreth = 0x2AC

   // ---- D2: the mirror of C2 -- a non-retiring cycle must NOT roll minstret over ----
m4_asm(CSRRW, x0,  x2,  101100000010)        // 140  minstret <= 0xFFFFFFFF
m4_asm(EBREAK)                               // 144  TRAP 4, cause 3. minstret must HOLD, so the
                                             //      carry waits for the handler's first instruction
m4_asm(CSRRS, x31, x0,  101110000010)        // 148  x31 = minstreth = 0x2AD (exactly one carry)

m4_asm(WFI)                                  // 152  must decode as a legal NOP, must NOT trap
m4_asm(ADDI,  x19, x0,  101010)              // 156  x19 = 42, sentinel: main path completed
m4_asm(BGE,   x0,  x0,  0)                   // 160  halt

   // ---- shared handler ----
m4_asm(CSRRS, x22, x0,  1101000001)          // 164  handler: x22 = mepc
m4_asm(CSRRS, x23, x0,  1101000010)          // 168  x23 = mcause
m4_asm(ADD,   x20, x20, x23)                 // 172  x20 += mcause
m4_asm(ADDI,  x21, x21, 1)                   // 176  x21 += 1 (trap counter)
m4_asm(ADDI,  x25, x22, 100)                 // 180  x25 = mepc + 4
m4_asm(CSRRW, x0,  x25, 1101000001)          // 184  mepc = mepc + 4
m4_asm(MRET)                                 // 188  -> PC = mepc
m4_asm(ADDI,  x30, x0,  1111100111)          // 192  poison (999): must NEVER execute
m4_asm(BGE,   x0,  x0,  0)                   // 196  handler halt (unreachable)
m4_asm_end()


Every counter probe here works by SEEDING the low half to 0xFFFFFFFF with a CSR write and then
letting exactly one cycle elapse, so the rollover lands on an instruction this program chose. That
is the only way to reach a 2^32 boundary in a 250-cycle simulation. It also means every expected
value below is exact, not approximate: a CSR read returns $..._old, i.e. the value one cycle after
the write, so mcycle read immediately after "mcycle <= 0x100" is 0x101, deterministically.

mcycleh starts at 0x2AB (written at 72) and is expected to reach 0x2AD via two carries. A nonzero
base is deliberate -- it distinguishes "the carry incremented the high half" from "something set
the high half to 1".

Expected Waveforms:
Reg	Value		Why
x1	000000A4	164, the handler address
x2	FFFFFFFF	the rollover seed written into mcycle/minstret four times
x3	00000100	0x100
x4	000002AB	0x2AB, the high-half seed
x5	00000000	mhpmcounter3 -- hardwired zero
x6	00000000	mhpmcounter31 -- the top of the 0xB03-0xB1F range
x7	00000000	mhpmcounterh3
x8	00000000	mhpmcounterh31 -- top of 0xB83-0xB9F
x9	00000000	mhpmevent3
x10	00000000	mhpmevent31 -- top of 0x323-0x33F
x11	00000000	0xB03 after writing 0xFFFFFFFF to it. Nonzero here means you gave it real storage; a trap here instead means the block was wrongly classified read-only
x12	0000022B	555 canary UNCHANGED -- the read of 0xB01 trapped. Without the canary this is indistinguishable from a successful read of a hardwired-zero CSR, which is exactly the trap testcodes/11 documented
x13	0000022B	555 canary UNCHANGED -- the read of 0x320 (mcountinhibit, deliberately not implemented) trapped
x14	00000101	mcycle read one cycle after being written to 0x100. Proves the mcycleh write at 72 did NOT land in the low half -- a merged write would show 000002AB or 000002AC here
x15	000002AB	mcycleh read back. Proves the high half has real storage and its own write port
x16	000002AB	minstreth read back, same for the instruction counter
x17	00000001	the dummy retiring instruction that carries mcycle in C1
x18	000002AC	FIRST carry: mcycleh incremented by one on the rollover. Not 1 -- an increment, not a set
x19	0000002A	42 -- sentinel proving the main path reached the end
x20	00000012	18 = sum of mcause over four traps: 2 + 2 + 11 + 3
x21	00000004	THE trap count: exactly the two deliberate boundary traps plus ECALL plus EBREAK
x22	00000090	144 -- mepc of the LAST trap (the EBREAK)
x23	00000003	mcause of the LAST trap = 3, breakpoint
x25	00000094	148 -- mepc + 4 from the last trap
x26	000002AD	THE probe. Second carry, taken on the ECALL's cycle. The ECALL does not retire, but the CYCLE elapsed, so mcycle rolled over and the carry is owed. A value of 000002AC means $mcycle_carry is gated on $retires
x27	000002AD	unchanged after C3 -- writing 0xFFFFFFFF into mcycle is not a rollover. 000002AE means the carry is missing its !$mcycle_wr qualifier
x28	00000001	the dummy retiring instruction that carries minstret in D1
x29	000002AC	minstreth after exactly one carry
x30	00000000	poison never written
x31	000002AD	minstreth after D2: exactly ONE further carry, taken by the handler's first instruction, not by the EBREAK. See the note below -- the failure value here is dramatic

Notes:
- Read the two carry probes as a matched pair. They are the same experiment run against the two
  counters, and the correct answer has OPPOSITE polarity:
      x26 (mcycle)   the rollover cycle is an ECALL that does not retire -> the carry MUST fire
      x31 (minstret) the rollover cycle is an EBREAK that does not retire -> the carry must NOT fire
  The reason is that the carry qualifier has to match the increment condition of the half beneath
  it. $mcycle increments unconditionally every cycle, so its carry is (!write && old==FFFFFFFF) and
  nothing more. $minstret increments only on $retires, so its carry needs $retires too. Copying one
  line to the other is the natural mistake and this pair is built to catch it in both directions.

- x31's failure mode is loud rather than off-by-one. If $minstret_carry drops its $retires term,
  minstret sits at 0xFFFFFFFF across the EBREAK, both shadow instructions AND the trap entry, and
  the carry fires on every one of those cycles. Expect 000002B0 rather than 000002AD. A value one
  greater than expected means something else; a value three greater means this.

- x26's arithmetic does NOT depend on the handler length, unlike testcodes/13's group G. The carry
  is taken by the ECALL's own cycle, before the handler is entered. x31's does not depend on it
  either -- the carry is taken by whichever instruction retires first inside the handler, and there
  is always exactly one such instruction. You can lengthen or shorten the handler without
  invalidating this file.

- If mcycleh/minstreth are missing from $csr_addr_valid, this test does not fail gracefully: the
  CSRRW at 72 raises illegal-instruction, the handler skips it, and every subsequent counter probe
  traps as well. Symptom is x21 in the teens with x15, x16, x18, x26, x27, x29 and x31 all zero.
  Check $csr_addr_valid and the $csr_read_data mux before reading anything else in this table. Note
  that the >= 3 term in the mhpmcounterh range decode deliberately excludes 0xB80 and 0xB82, so
  they need explicit entries -- the range does not cover them.

- Group A' is the boundary, and it is the half of the range decode that is easy to leave untested.
  0xB03 must be legal while 0xB01 traps; 0x323 must be legal while 0x320 traps. A decode written as
  a bare prefix match with no low-bit condition passes every probe in group A and fails here, with
  x21 = 2 and x12 = x13 = 00000000.

- x14 is the isolation probe and it is exact, not approximate. mcycle is written to 0x100 at 68, the
  mcycleh write at 72 lets it tick once, and the read at 76 returns $mcycle_old = 0x101. If the two
  CSR addresses are not distinguished at the write port this reads 000002AB or 000002AC.

- WFI at 152 is not a Tier 5 item; it is here because it costs one instruction and it is the last
  known decode gap. If it traps, x21 = 5 and x23 = 00000002. Remember it needs to be excluded from
  the SYSTEM allowlist inside $illegal_instr, not just added to $decode_valid -- those are two
  independent terms and satisfying one does not satisfy the other.

- NOT covered: the 64-bit read tearing window (a software problem -- the ISA's answer is the
  read-high / read-low / read-high-again retry loop, and hardware is required to do nothing about
  it), mcycle's absolute value at any point (it is a property of your pipeline, not of the
  architecture, and can never be signature-compared against a reference), and mcountinhibit, which
  is deliberately unimplemented and is verified here only by the fact that it traps.
